# converted from 07-the-boy.md
@title The Boy
@bg mire
@mood uneasy

narrate He watched him for eleven weeks.
narrate That was the part that would have frightened Corin most, if anyone had ever told him, and nobody ever did. Cael was in the reeds at Sallow's Rest. Cael was on the ridge above the eastern line when the boy stepped over the rope. Cael was in the trees outside the Hollow on the night of the Fever, and in the shrine yard on the morning after, and on the road north, and — once, for four hours, in the rain, without moving — outside Maeve's cottage, watching a lamp in a window and a silhouette that did not sit down.
narrate He told himself he was assessing a threat.
narrate He had been telling himself things for eleven years. He was very good at it.
narrate The truth was simpler and worse.
narrate He was dying.
narrate Not quickly. The mark had been on his palm for eleven years and it had been taking, steadily, the way a river takes a bank — and there was not much left. He had perhaps five years, perhaps fewer, and when it finished there would be a hole in the world where the only person who could end the Grey Fever used to be.
narrate He had spent eleven years telling himself he was waiting for the right moment. He had spent eleven years being a monster so that when the moment came he would be ready — hard enough, empty enough, to pay the price without flinching.
narrate And every year the price got larger, because every year he had more to lose.
narrate He was not waiting for the right moment. He was waiting for someone else to do it.
narrate The tea was a test.
narrate He wanted that understood, at least by himself. When the boy walked into the burned kitchen with a dog and stood in the doorway looking at a cushion, Cael had three plans and none of them were tea.
narrate He made tea because he wanted to see what the boy would do with it.
narrate Because a person who has walked into the house of the man they have been sent to kill, and been handed a cup by that man, will do exactly one of four things: they will refuse it, they will drink it and stay standing, they will drink it and sit down, or they will ask what it cost.
narrate Corin drank it.
narrate And then — and this was the moment, this was the whole of it, this was the reason Cael would spend the next eleven weeks arranging his own destruction — Corin sat down, and put both hands round the cup the way you do when you are cold, and said:
corin neutral There's no milk.
narrate Cael had to leave the room.
narrate He said something about the fire and he went outside and stood in the herb garden with his hands on his knees, because a nineteen-year-old soldier had sat down in his kitchen, in his daughter's house, in the chair, and had said the most ordinary thing in the world, and Cael had heard it in his own voice.
narrate He stood in the garden for four minutes.
narrate Then he went back in and told a boy a story about a cushion, and watched him not flinch, and understood — with the particular clarity of a man who has been waiting eleven years — that he had found him.
narrate He tested him for eleven weeks.
narrate He tested him at Sallow's Rest, before he ever met him — standing in the trees, watching, doing nothing, waiting to see whether the boy would get up. He got up.
narrate He tested him at the eastern line, in the rain, from the ridge: whether the boy would step over the rope for two strangers. He stepped.
narrate He tested him in the shrine, on the seventh night of the Fever, when Maeve put two beds and three people in front of him. He chose.
narrate And every time, Cael stood somewhere in the dark with his hand on a trunk and watched a nineteen-year-old do the thing he himself had failed to do at that age, and felt the same thing he had felt in the trees at Sallow's Rest.
narrate Envy. And under it, quietly, the other thing, the thing he would not name until a field eleven weeks later.
narrate He knew what he was doing. He wanted that understood too.
narrate He was not grooming a successor. That word was too kind and too simple. What Cael was doing was worse and stranger: he was raising one. From a distance, without permission, the way you might tend a garden you have no right to.
narrate He was giving the boy a monster to hate, because a boy needs something to be against, and he was giving him a villain with a sad story because that is the only kind of villain who teaches anything.
narrate And he was arranging, carefully, over eleven weeks, for that boy to end up standing at the edge of a hole in a field with a mark on his palm and a decision to make — so that when the moment came, somebody would be there.
narrate Somebody who had not spent eleven years being ready.
narrate Somebody who would just do it.
narrate There was one night he nearly stopped.
narrate The night of the Hollow. He was three miles north with his hands in a woman's chest, doing the only thing he had ever actually been good at, and he heard the Hollow burn — he heard it the way he heard everything, the thinning, the wrongness — and he knew that the boy was in there, and that if the boy died in that shrine he would have wasted eleven years and forty-one villages and the only thing he had left.
narrate He could have gone south. He had two hours and he could have spent them going south, and saved the boy, and let the family die.
narrate He stayed.
narrate He told himself afterwards that it was the arithmetic — four lives against one, and the boy was militia, the boy had a chance. He told the boy that, in the reeds, the next morning, and the boy hit him for it.
narrate He told the boy the truth. He was proud of that.
narrate What he did not tell the boy — what he did not tell anyone, ever, and what he would think about in the last chapter of his life — was the actual reason.
narrate He had not gone south because going south would have meant choosing the boy.
narrate And he had spent eleven years arranging for the boy to choose himself.
